lon d\\) we can obtain a lossless expander.\n\n▶ **Lemma 3.2.** Let \\(f \\in \\mathbb{F}_{q^n}[X]\\) be a \\(q\\)-linearized polynomial with \\(q\\)-degree at most \\(k-1\\). Let \\(V \\subseteq \\mathbb{F}_{q^n}\\) be an \\(\\mathbb{F}_q\\)-subspace, and \\(Q \\in \\mathcal{L}_{D,s}\\), a polynomial satisfying (2). Suppose that \\(f(\\alpha) \\in V\\) for all \\(\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{F}_h=\\mathbb{F}_{q^d}\\) and that \\(D \\le d-k+1\\). Then\n\n\\[\nA_0(f(X)) + A_1(f^\\sigma(X)) + \\cdots + A_{s-1}(f^{\\sigma^{s-1}}(X))\n= Q(f(X),f^\\sigma(X),\\ldots,f^{\\sigma^{s-1}}(X)) = 0.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n▶ **Lemma 3.3.** The set of solutions to Equation (3), for any nonzero \\(Q \\in \\mathcal{L}_{D,s}\\) (for arbitrary \\(D\\)), is an \\((s-1,d)\\)-periodic subspace.\n\nEquipped with these lemmas, we are in position to deduce our main theorem for this section.\n\n▶ **Theorem 3.4.** Let \\(\\{H_i\\}_{i=0}^{k-1}\\) give a \\((s,A,d)\\)-subspace design for all \\(s \\le \\mu n\\) for some \\(0<\\mu<1/d\\). Then \\(\\{\\Gamma_j\\}_{j=1}^d\\) is a \\((\\mu A,\\frac{d-k+1}{d})\\)-dimension expander. Moreover if the subspace design is explicit then the dimension expander is explicit.\n\nThus, we have that subspaces of dimension \\(As\\) are expanded to subspaces of dimension \\((d-k+1)s/A\\). This informs what we should hope for from subspace designs. In particular, obtaining \\(A=O(1)\\) is enough to obtain a degree proportional expander (by setting \\(k=\\Theta(d)\\)), while if \\(A\\approx 1+\\varepsilon\\) and \\(k\\approx \\varepsilon d\\) we can obtain a lossless expander. With these goals in mind, we turn our attention to constructing subspace designs.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nElectronic Colloquium on Computational Complexity, Report No. 162 (2014) (https://eccc.weizmann.ac.il/report/2014/162/download)\nciteturn0search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; codes thereby yield lossless two-source condensers wit